* sync.Mutex

We've seen how channels are great for communication among goroutines.

But what if we don't need communication? What if we just want to make sure only
one ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000001⟫ can access a variable at a time to avoid conflicts?

This concept is called ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000002⟫mutual_exclusion⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000003⟫, and the conventional name for the data structure that provides it is ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000004⟫mutex⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000005⟫.

⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000006⟫'s standard library provides mutual exclusion with
[[⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000007⟫][⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000008⟫sync.Mutex⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000009⟫]] and its two methods:

- ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000010⟫Lock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000011⟫
- ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000012⟫Unlock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000013⟫

We can define a block of code to be executed in mutual exclusion by surrounding it
with a call to ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000014⟫Lock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000015⟫ and ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000016⟫Unlock⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000017⟫ as shown on the ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000018⟫Inc⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000019⟫ method.

We can also use ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000020⟫defer⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000021⟫ to ensure the mutex will be unlocked as in the ⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000022⟫Value⟪GTI18N_49f72779_000023⟫ method.

